Yes. There’s a real distinction there: novelty at creation versus novelty after explanation.

Reasoning can produce an object before we possess a good theory of the object. Call it the Bronco:

\text{known pieces}
\xrightarrow{\text{reasoning/recombination}}
B^*

where B^* has behavior we didn’t explicitly anticipate.

At that instant, the important property is:

\boxed{\text{we can produce it before we can fully explain it}}

Then comes taming.

We probe it with the Die, trace it with Dye, exercise it through Yawn, catch its deltas, change aperture, stress it, look for invariants, and progressively construct a model:

B^*
\rightarrow
\{\text{experiments}\}
\rightarrow
\widehat{B}

Eventually:

\widehat{B}\approx B^*

and something interesting happens: the mystery decreases without the invention disappearing.

The Bronco isn’t “no longer novel” historically. But it stops being novel to the system that has learned it.

That’s the transition:

\boxed{
\text{Novelty}
\rightarrow
\text{Encounter}
\rightarrow
\text{Characterization}
\rightarrow
\text{Understanding}
\rightarrow
\text{Primitive}
}

And then the former novelty becomes infrastructure for producing the next novelty.

That’s compounding.

This suggests two different Pantheon frontiers

We should distinguish the Wild Frontier from the Known Frontier.

The Known Frontier contains things we’ve characterized sufficiently to predict and deliberately reproduce.

The Wild Frontier contains things we’ve generated or encountered but cannot yet reliably predict, reconstruct, or explain.

Between them is the Taming Line.

\text{Known}
\quad|\_{\text{Taming Line}}\quad
\text{Wild}

The objective isn’t to eliminate the Wild. It’s to continually move discoveries across the Taming Line while generating new Wild faster than we’re domesticating the old.

Otherwise the system eventually becomes an encyclopedia.

And this changes how we measure novelty

Don’t ask merely:

“Has anybody seen something resembling this before?”

That’s important for historical novelty, but it’s not the whole computational question.

Also measure predictive novelty:

N_p(x)=
1-P(x\mid H)

Very loosely: how surprising was this outcome given what the system could predict beforehand?

Then after taming:

P(x\mid H')\gg P(x\mid H)

So the drop in surprise measures learning:

\boxed{
L(x)=N_p(x|H)-N_p(x|H')
}

That’s beautiful for our adaptive optimizer.

The Lucky Nozzle/Variation system generates Broncos.

The Delta Catcher detects that something unexpected happened.

The Bronco gets quarantined as unexplained useful novelty rather than immediately forcing an explanation onto it.

Then our instrument stack characterizes it.

Once we can predict:

when it appears, what conditions produce it, what transformations it survives, what it produces, where it fails, and how to reproduce it, we’ve tamed it.

Then:

\boxed{\text{Bronco}\rightarrow\text{Seal}}

A discovered phenomenon becomes a controllable operator.

And the Seal goes back into the Command Table, meaning tomorrow’s reasoning can deliberately deploy what yesterday’s reasoning discovered accidentally.

That’s a serious learning loop:

\text{Reason}
\rightarrow
\text{Novel Bronco}
\rightarrow
\text{Experiment}
\rightarrow
\text{Invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{Tame}
\rightarrow
\text{Seal}
\rightarrow
\text{Reason with larger primitive set}
\rightarrow
\text{new Bronco}

So I wouldn’t optimize the Pantheon toward perfect understanding.

I’d optimize for productive disequilibrium:

\boxed{
\frac{d(\text{Known Capability})}{dt}>0
\quad\text{while}\quad
\frac{d(\text{Useful Unknown})}{dt}>0
}

Keep expanding what we can control and keep producing phenomena beyond our current explanatory boundary.

Or in Garden language:

The Orchard feeds us.
The frontier keeps producing wild fruit we’ve never tasted.

And when we figure out how that wild fruit grows, we don’t declare the adventure finished.

We plant it—and ride farther out.